  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">NIHR Global Health Research Group on Physical Trauma from Injury &amp; POsT ConflicT; iPrOTeCT</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Physical trauma resulting in serious, life changing injuries is endemic in Low- and Middle-Income Countries (LMICs). This project builds on prior work through an understanding of the broader global burden of physical trauma from conflict, non-conflict events such as terror attacks and the growing burden of road traffic accidents due to rapid increases in urbanisation and motor vehicle use. This project works across three continents and will collaborate with some of the largest humanitarian organisations to make change happen. Imperial College London, UK is Co-director of the project and will work on most of the aims. University of Global Health Equity, Rwanda, is the other Co-director and will head the translation activities there with the help of the University of Rwanda. The American University of Beirut, Lebanon, leads on the burden of injury work in the 2006 Lebanon war and gunshot wound project in Gaza. Finally, University of Moratuwa, Sri Lanka, are working on regenerative medicine aspects within the project.</narrative>
  </description>
